{"kind":"request","text":"LS-5: find why the proxy's reply to `install` is no answer on Linux.\n\nThe owner agreed a diagnostics route for workflows. I pushed the\napproved head `5784e23d` to the branch `diag/smoke-20261115` and\ndispatched Local Worker smoke on it. Run 43781291767 failed in 50 s,\nand the LS-3 classifier reports:\n\n- `owner: proxy`, `faultBoundary: proxy-forwarding`.\n- `operation: install`, `detail: transport-no-answer`,\n  `transport.kind: reply-not-answer`, status `4xx`.\n- The exchange completed on both sides: `proxyDecision: forwarded`,\n  `upstreamStatus: 4xx`, `clientStatus: 4xx`.\n- `startupHealth: http-503`, `startupSessions: ok`.\n\nWork:\n\n1. State from the source which 4xx replies the Worker or celld can\n   give to this request, and why the classifier names the proxy when\n   the upstream sent the status.\n2. Add to the report the exact status code and a closed class of\n   the reply body. Print no body text and no header value.\n3. Say what `startupHealth: http-503` means for the run, and whether\n   the smoke should wait for health before `install`.\n4. Fix the cause when the diagnostics name it.\n\nThe route: send me a head, and I push it to a `diag/` branch and\ndispatch the workflow. A run takes about one minute. A diagnostic\nhead that changes only `scripts/celld-smoke*` and\n`scripts/test-celld-proxy*` needs no gate and no review, and it\nnever lands. The fix lands by the ordinary route with one gate.\nThe repository is public, so a diagnostic prints no secret and no\nprivate path.","body":{"conditions":"The smoke report states the exact status and a closed body class; the cause is named from a workflow run; the fix lands with one gate and a green Local Worker smoke run","target_head":"9e42735a1901b54c09145841dae51439f522b1c1","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}